**Definition 1.4 (All fifteen invariant coefficients).** For orthogonal
unit vectors let $F(a,b)=(ab^T+ba^T)/\sqrt2$. Use exactly QRC
Definition 4.1:

$$
H_i=R_i/\sqrt{n_i},\qquad
\tau=(1,\sqrt2,\sqrt2)^T,\qquad I=\sum_i\tau_iH_i,
\tag{1.5}
$$

and the following six ordered orthonormal matrix pairs:

$$
\begin{aligned}
+ &: F(d,u_+),\quad F(d,v_+),\\
- &: F(d,u_-),\quad F(d,v_-),\\
2+ &: (u_+u_+^T-v_+v_+^T)/\sqrt2,\quad F(u_+,v_+),\\
2- &: (u_-u_-^T-v_-v_-^T)/\sqrt2,\quad F(u_-,v_-),\\
\Delta &: [F(u_+,u_-)+F(v_+,v_-)]/\sqrt2,\quad
          [F(v_+,u_-)-F(u_+,v_-)]/\sqrt2,\\
\Sigma &: [F(u_+,u_-)-F(v_+,v_-)]/\sqrt2,\quad
          [F(v_+,u_-)+F(u_+,v_-)]/\sqrt2.
\end{aligned}
\tag{1.6}
$$

Denote the pair by $(F_{\nu1},F_{\nu2})$ and its label set by
$\mathcal N=\{+,-,2+,2-,\Delta,\Sigma\}$. Define, also for
unnormalized real vectors,

$$
\begin{gathered}
h_i(a)=a^TH_ia,\qquad
z_\nu(a)=(a^TF_{\nu1}a,a^TF_{\nu2}a)^T,\\
k(a,b)=\left(h(a)h(b)^T,
       \left(\tfrac12z_\nu(a)^Tz_\nu(b)\right)_{\nu\in\mathcal N}\right),\\
\mathcal K=\operatorname{cone}\{k(a,b):a,b\in\mathbb R^5,
                                  \ \|a\|=\|b\|=1\},\\
P_\nu(E)=\sum_{j=1}^2\operatorname{tr}(F_{\nu j}E)F_{\nu j}.
\end{gathered}
\tag{1.7}
$$

Cone means finite nonnegative sums. In particular the preparation
factor $a$ and measurement factor $b$ are independent. The homogeneous
extension satisfies $k(sa,tb)=s^2t^2k(a,b)$ for real $s,t$.
For $x=(M,\eta)$ define the complex-linear map, first on real
symmetric matrices,

$$
S_x(E)=\sum_{i,j}M_{ij}\operatorname{tr}(H_jE)H_i
             +\sum_{\nu\in\mathcal N}\eta_\nu P_\nu(E),
\tag{1.8}
$$

and extend after replacing a complex matrix $X$ by $(X+X^T)/2$.
Its unnormalized output-first Choi matrix is

$$
Z(x)=\sum_{i,j}M_{ij}H_i\otimes H_j
 +\sum_\nu\eta_\nu(F_{\nu1}\otimes F_{\nu1}
                         +F_{\nu2}\otimes F_{\nu2}).
\tag{1.9}
$$

QRC Theorems 4.2 and 5.2 identify the real stationary reflected
measure-and-prepare channels with

$$
\mathcal F=\{(M,\eta)\in\mathcal K:M^T\tau=\tau\},\qquad
\operatorname{Tr}_{\rm out}(Z(x)/5)=I_5/5.
\tag{1.10}
$$

The correspondence is injective on all fifteen coefficients and
determines the action on every complex input. The three TP equations
are independent. Neither $M=M^T$ nor $M\tau=\tau$ is imposed.
Their imposition would add self-adjointness or unitality, respectively.
The general rank-one-Kraus, separable-Choi and measure-and-prepare
correspondence is the credited ingredient of
[Horodecki–Shor–Ruskai, Theorem 4(A–D)](https://arxiv.org/html/quant-ph/0302031v2),
whose CPTP version separately requires the measurement normalization.
Here that normalization is the full marginal (1.10).

